<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Grant (British Army officer)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1720">1720</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as James Grant of Ballindalloch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Grant was a Scottish army officer who served as the first governor of British East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> from 1764 to 1771, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded Florida in 1763. He tried to attract settlers with land grants and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> plantations. He later commanded British troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-grant-british-army-officer.jpg" alt="James Grant (British Army officer)" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Life time: 18th Century) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Major_General_James_Grant,_circa_1770.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grant was born on the family estate of Ballindalloch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banffshire">Banffshire</a>, in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. He bought a captain's commission in the Royal Scots on 24 October 1744 and fought with the regiment at the Battle of Fontenoy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>. By 1757 he was a major of the 77th Regiment of Foot (Montgomerie's Highlanders) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. On General John Forbes's expedition he led an advance party of around 800 men towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a>, split his force and was himself ambushed by Indians and French on 14 September 1758, losing 342 men killed, wounded or captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Taken prisoner and soon paroled, he blamed his defeat on the colonial militia. He commanded an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> in 1761 and fought at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. As governor of East Florida, living in the Governor's House at St. Augustine, he made the Treaty of Fort Picolata to end Indian raids, drawing on the gift exchange he had learned among Southeast Indian groups, and established the Florida-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> border. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and cochineal and developed plantations of his own, among them Grant's Villa, worked by enslaved Africans. Illness forced his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1771.</p><p>Elected to Parliament for Tain Burghs in 1773, he spoke against the colonists, saying in 1775 that they could not fight. He returned to service that summer, commanded the 55th Regiment of Foot from December 1775 to 1791, and became General William Howe's chief planning officer in the New York Campaign, leading the left wing at the Battle of Long Island on 26 and 27 August. He devised the plans for the Battle of Brandywine Creek in 1777, failed to trap the marquis de Lafayette at Barren Hill on 20 May 1778, and in October led the force that took St. Lucia from the French.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Defeated at the election of 1780, he became a lieutenant general in 1782 and sat for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a> from 1787 to 1802. In 1789 he was made governor of Stirling Castle and commander of the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and in 1796 a full general. He retired to Ballindalloch as its laird in 1802, left the army in 1805 and died on 13 April 1806, aged 86. His estate passed to his grandnephew, George Macpherson; his papers are at the National Archives of Scotland and have been copied for the Library of Congress.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Grant (British Army officer, born 1720)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal governors, first <b>James Grant</b> and later Patrick Tonyn, tried to attract settlers with land grants.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-east-florida">Province of East Florida</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ballindalloch, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.41,-3.37&amp;year=1775" aria-label="Ballindalloch on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4478 219 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Grant (British Army officer)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4928 489h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4928 489h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.41,-3.37&amp;year=1775">What was here in 1775? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
